# ShEx

```elixir
Mix.install(rudof_elixir: [path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)], kino: "== 0.19.0")
```

## Introduction

This document contains a short introduction to ShEx using [rudof](https://rudof-project.github.io/).

## Preliminaries: Install and configure rudof

First, we install and configure `rudof`. The setup cell installs the Elixir wrapper and creates a native session.

```elixir
alias Rudof.Session
import ExUnit.Assertions
rudof = Session.new!()
```

## Validating RDF using ShEx

[ShEx (Shape Expressions)](https://shex.io/) is a concise and human-readable language to describe and validate RDF data.

A ShEx schema contains several declarations of shapes and can be defined in several formats: a compact format (ShExC), a JSON-LD format (ShExJ) and an RDF based on (ShExR).

Let's start defining a simple ShEx schema as:

```elixir
:ok =
  Session.read_shex(rudof, ~S"""
  prefix :     <http://example.org/>
  prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

  :User {
   :name      xsd:string   ;
   :birthDate xsd:date     ;
   :knows     @:User     * ;
   :worksFor  @:Company  *
  }

  :Company {
    :name     xsd:string    ;
    :code     xsd:integer   ;
    :employee @:User      *
  }
  """)
```

And let's define some RDF data.

```elixir
:ok =
  Session.read_data(
    rudof,
    ~S"""
    prefix : <http://example.org/>
    prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
    prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

    :alice a :Person ;
     :name      "Alice"                ;
     :birthDate "2005-03-01"^^xsd:date ;
     :worksFor  :acme                  ;
     :knows     :bob                   .
    :bob a :Person   ;
     :name      "Robert Smith"         ;
     :birthDate "2003-01-02"^^xsd:date ;
     :worksFor  :acme                  ;
     :knows     :alice                 .
    :acme  a :Company  ;
     :name      "Acme Inc." ;
     :code      23          .
    """)
```

In order to validate nodes in a graph, ShEx uses a [ShapeMap](https://shex.io/shape-map/), which associates a node selector with a shape.

The simplest shape map is a pair `node@shape`.

Rudof keeps the current shape map in a structure so it can be reused.

The method `read_shapemap(shapemap)` can be used to read a shapemap from a string and store it's value as the currrent shape map.

In the previous example, if we want to validate `:alice` as a `:Person` we can use:

In this Elixir example the declared shape is `:User`, so the selector below uses `:User`; `Session.read_shapemap/2` is the direct wrapper for `read_shapemap(shapemap)`.

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_shapemap(rudof, ":alice@:User")
```

Once the ShEx schema and the Shapemap have been added to `rudof`, it is possible to validate the current RDF data with the `validate_shex()` method:

The Elixir wrapper exposes this as `Session.validate_shex/1` and returns a report with the validation results.

```elixir
{:ok, results} = Session.validate_shex(rudof)
IO.inspect(results)
assert results.conforms
results
```

If we want to validate `:acme` as a `:Company`, we could do:

The upstream code below selects `:alice` as in the original notebook, so this remains a non-conformant example.

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_shapemap(rudof, ":alice@:Company")
{:ok, results} = Session.validate_shex(rudof)
IO.inspect(results)
refute results.conforms
results
```

The shapemap can contain a list of nodes and shapes, so it is possible to run several validations like:

```elixir
:ok = Session.read_shapemap(rudof, ":alice@:User, :bob@:User")
{:ok, results} = Session.validate_shex(rudof)
IO.inspect(results)
assert results.conforms
results
```

We reset the status of the `ShEx` schema, the `Shapemap` and the current `RDF data` for the next section.

## Validating SPARQL endpoints

It is also possible to validate RDF data which is not local but is available in a SPARQL endpoint like wikidata or dbpedia. Let's start with Wikidata:

This example uses `Session.with_session/1` so the endpoint data, schema and ShapeMap are separate from the local examples.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

We can declare a simple shape in Wikidata as follows:

```elixir
wikidata_endpoint = "https://query.wikidata.org/sparql"

wikidata_schema = ~S"""
PREFIX : <http://example.org/>
PREFIX wd: <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/>
PREFIX wdt: <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/>
:Researcher { wdt:P31 [ wd:Q5 ] ; wdt:P19 @:Place }
:Place { wdt:P17 @:Country * }
:Country {}
"""

```

```elixir
Session.with_session(fn remote ->
  :ok = Session.read_endpoint(remote, wikidata_endpoint)
  :ok = Session.read_shex(remote, wikidata_schema)
  :ok = Session.read_shapemap(remote, "wd:Q80@:Researcher")
  {:ok, report} = Session.validate_shex(remote)
  Kino.DataTable.new(report.entries)
end)
```

## Visualizing ShEx schemas

`rudof` can be used to convert ShEx to diagrams in UML-like style. The converter generates a [PlantUML](https://plantuml.com/) string which can be written to a file and converted to an image using the PlantUML tool.

Now we install the PlantUML tools necessary to process the generated `plant_uml`. In this Livebook, `Rudof.Visualization.plantuml_to_mermaid/1` adapts the generated string for Livebook's Mermaid renderer, so no PlantUML installation is needed.

```elixir
shex_schema = ~S"""
prefix : <http://example.org/>
prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

:User {
 :name     xsd:string  ;
 :worksFor @:Company * ;
 :addres   @:Address   ;
 :knows    @:User
}

:Company {
  :name     xsd:string     ;
  :code     xsd:string     ;
  :employee @:User
}
:Address {
  :name     xsd:string ;
  :zip_code xsd:string
}
"""
```

```elixir
{:ok, plantuml} =
  Session.show_schema_conversion(rudof, shex_schema, :shex, :uml,
    input_format: :shexc,
    output_format: :uml,
    viz_engine: :plantuml
  )

IO.puts(plantuml)
```

```elixir
{:ok, diagram} = Rudof.Visualization.plantuml_to_mermaid(plantuml)
Kino.Mermaid.new(diagram)
```

`Kino.Mermaid.new/1` renders the converted diagram directly in Livebook.

## Close the session

Release the native state when finished. To revisit earlier cells, evaluate from the beginning.

```elixir
:ok = Session.close(rudof)
```
